      "artist": "shankar s. from Dubai, united arab emirates",
      "description": "We had a long day ahead of us, so a few quick shots of Penang's classic buildings before we head on. At any rate, I do not like to spend much time in religious places, so a quick shot from outside is usually it. This beautiful structure is the St. George's church in Penang. The church was originally built in 1817 using convict labour and with the help of the British East India Company. This church is historically significant in this part of the world because it is the oldest Anglican Church in Southeast Asia. The most striking feature of the church's architecture are without a doubt the huge Grecian columns lined outside the front entrance. The church was badly damaged during the war, especially the interior which was vandalised, and was restored in 1948. (Georgetown, Penang, Malaysia, Nov. 2013)",
